{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the 12th century known for his military prowess and political acumen. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1102 until his death in 1153. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's territory and influence.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county at a young age following his father's death in 1102. He quickly established himself as a capable ruler and began to assert Tripoli's independence from the Byzantine Empire. He faced challenges from both the Byzantines and the Normans who sought to control the region. Raymond III skillfully navigated these complex political landscapes.\n\nHis reign saw a considerable increase in Tripoli's power and wealth. He fostered trade particularly with Venice and Genoa which brought prosperity to the county. He also strengthened Tripoli's military by expanding its army and building fortifications. Raymond III’s governance was characterized by a blend of piety and pragmatism. He was a devout Christian who actively participated in religious life but also a shrewd administrator who focused on practical matters of state.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III’s reign was defined by numerous military campaigns. He engaged in frequent conflicts with the Byzantine Empire over control of the Levant and the Aegean Sea. He successfully defended Tripoli against Byzantine attacks and expanded its territory into Anatolia. He also fought against the Normans who were expanding their influence in Southern Italy and Sicily. These conflicts often involved naval battles and land sieges. Raymond III was a skilled military leader and his campaigns were often characterized by innovative tactics. His victories established Tripoli as a major power in the Eastern Mediterranean. He 